config SERVICE_COREMMC
	bool "Secondary eMMC (fabric CoreMMC) support"
	default n
	help
                This feature enables support for the secondary eMMC device,
                which is attached to a CoreMMC instance in the FPGA fabric
                rather than to the hardened MSS MMC controller.

                It adds a boot source (selectable from the TinyCLI and from
                the Boot Parameters store as BOOT_SRC_SECONDARY), and lets
                the USBDMSC service export that device instead of the
                primary one.

                The device is reached one 512-byte block at a time - the
                CoreMMC instance has a 512-byte FIFO and no DMA path - so
                it is markedly slower than the primary eMMC.

		If you do not know what to do here, say N.

menu "Secondary eMMC (CoreMMC)"
	visible if SERVICE_COREMMC

config SERVICE_COREMMC_BASE_ADDR
	hex "CoreMMC instance base address"
	default 0xE0000000
	depends on SERVICE_COREMMC
	help
                Base address of the CoreMMC register file on the fabric AHB
                slave. The register file is 128 bytes deep and aliases
                throughout the bridge's slave range, so software must only
                access it at this base address.

                Must match BSP_COREMMC_BASE_ADDR in the application tree.

config SERVICE_COREMMC_CLK_RATE_DIV
	int "CoreMMC clock divider for data transfers"
	default 0
	range 0 255
	depends on SERVICE_COREMMC
	help
                MMC clock = HCLK / (2 * (this + 1)), where HCLK is the fabric
                AHB clock feeding the CoreMMC instance.

                Must match BSP_COREMMC_CLK_RATE_DIV in the application tree.

config SERVICE_COREMMC_INIT_CLK_RATE_DIV
	int "CoreMMC clock divider during device initialization"
	default 49
	range 0 255
	depends on SERVICE_COREMMC
	help
                The eMMC clock must not exceed 400 kHz while the device is
                being identified. MMC clock = HCLK / (2 * (this + 1)).

                Must match BSP_COREMMC_INIT_CLK_RATE_DIV in the application
                tree.

config SERVICE_COREMMC_DATA_TIMEOUT
	hex "CoreMMC data timeout, in HCLK cycles"
	default 0x7FFFFFFF
	depends on SERVICE_COREMMC
	help
                How long the CoreMMC hardware waits on a data transfer before
                flagging a timeout. This is what ends a transfer to a device
                that has stopped answering: the driver's own poll loops are
                not otherwise bounded.

endmenu
